{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792838097,"nonce":"KYkMgI150yIUTXsVy1mgM8HKhO8=","title":"A root-package typedef shares its symbol with the type it aliases","message":"`lower_typedef_declaration` reuses the root-scope symbol for a typedef declared in the root package. std's `typedef Map\u003cK, V\u003e = haxe.ds.Map\u003cK, V\u003e` and `typedef List\u003cT\u003e = haxe.ds.List\u003cT\u003e` therefore land on the symbol of the abstract/class they alias, and the alias type is stamped onto that symbol. Member dispatch on `Map` depends on this: giving the root `Map` typedef its own symbol (as efd48e20 did for every typedef) lost TestMapComprehension and the MapComprehensionEntries fixture, and `resolve_type_to_class_symbol` needs a cycle guard because the class's own symbol is typed as an alias of itself.\n\nA typedef should own its symbol in every package, with dispatch resolving through the alias to its target. Named-package typedefs already do; the root package keeps the shared slot until Map/List dispatch reads through aliases.","files":null}]}